Albert Stillman was buried here barely  an hour ago his family says he died from  covert 19 they wanted to bury him at  their home and soon duza in rural  Eastern Cape but lockdown restrictions  mean this is his final resting place not  in a cemetery stared a solitary grave in  an open field to comply with  restrictions just a few people attended  the funeral the freedoms of tradition  and custom replaced by social distancing  rules they've never showed any respect  to my father even when he worked at the  mine he got nothing he died as a poor  man now he has been buried like garbage  because of the corona it is really  really painful the local council has his  33 people in this village have tested  positive for coronavirus but he thinks  the number is far higher we don't have  resources whereby people can test so  people just undermine it as a simple flu  they take it as a simple flu then after  a long time or after somebody's death  then it's right by they recognize this  is code 19 there is concern that  funerals which usually attract hundreds  of people are helping to spread the  virus at the cemetery outside the small  town of Berkeley East police Keep Watch  ensuring there are no more than 50  people attending the burial is short and  people leave quickly the eastern cape is  large much of it rural and people don't  live as close together compared to other  places so the spread of the corona virus  has surprised experts who were worried  this province may be south africa's next  epicenter some estimates say that as  many as 80% of the people here may get  covered 19 but in rural towns like this  one medical facilities are understaffed  and under resourced this hospital and  stack spread was closed when we filmed  him staff had protested against the lack  of personal protective equipment  shutting some of the units  authorities had restricted travel  between provinces to curb the outbreak  and permits are required to make a  journey here people are screened for the  virus we passed through the site twice  in two days  on the second day we were not screened  at all along with dozens of other  passengers in vehicles passing through  temperature checks and collecting  contact details only resumed once we  began filming while the number of covert  19 infections grow the government is  still easing some of its restrictions  and here in one of South Africa's  poorest provinces inconsistent plans and  policies may add to the rising numbers  Fermi tamela al Jazeera Eastern Cape  South Africa
 

